Google Auth Setup Guide — Hermes google-workspace
Generated 2026-09-26 · verified end-to-end on this install · portable to any Hermes agent

What this does
Makes the google-workspace skill (Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Sheets, Docs, Contacts) work on a machine where it is installed but not yet authenticated. It installs the pinned Google deps into a dedicated venv, walks the Google Cloud OAuth client, exchanges the code, and ends with an auto-refreshing token at ~/.hermes/google_token.json.

Two axes are independent and both must be right: APIs enabled on the Cloud project, and OAuth client credentials stored on the machine. An unenabled API fails later with 403: Access Not Configured.

Step 0 — Preflight (always run first)
The bundled setup.py pins google-auth==2.55.1, which needs Python ≥ 3.10. On a box whose only interpreter is 3.8/3.9 every pinned install fails, and running it with a bare python3 silently targets the wrong interpreter. The preflight script is idempotent and handles all of it:

sh "${HERMES_HOME:-$HOME/.hermes}/skills/productivity/google-oauth-setup/scripts/install-google-auth.sh"
picks the newest interpreter (3.13 → 3.10), or uses $HERMES_HOME/bin/uv to fetch one
creates $HERMES_HOME/google-workspace-venv
installs the pins via setup.py --install-deps run under that venv, so pins come from the script and cannot drift
writes $HERMES_HOME/bin/gsetup and gapi wrappers that always exec the venv
prints gsetup --check
Rule: use gsetup/gapi for every later call. Never system python3.

Step 1 — Triage
Which services? Email-only does not need this skill at all — use himalaya with a Gmail App Password (2 minutes, no Cloud project). This setup always requests the full scope set; it has no --services flag.
Advanced Protection? (hardware security keys). If yes, a Workspace admin must allowlist the OAuth client ID before consent works.
Step 2 — Google Cloud OAuth client (~5 min, user does this)
Create/select a project: console.cloud.google.com/projectselector2/home/dashboard
Enable APIs on that project: console.cloud.google.com/apis/library — Gmail API, Google Calendar API, Google Drive API, Google Sheets API, Google Docs API, People API
Create credentials: console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials → Create Credentials → OAuth 2.0 Client ID
Application type decides the redirect story:
Desktop app (recommended) — loopback redirects allowed automatically, nothing to register
Web application — the redirect URI must be registered byte-for-byte under the client’s Authorized redirect URIs, then passed as GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI
If the app is still Testing, add the account as a test user: console.cloud.google.com/auth/audience — otherwise consent dies with Error 403: access_denied
Download the client JSON, or copy the client ID + secret off the screen
Step 3 — Store the client secret
gsetup --client-secret /path/to/client_secret.json
If only raw values are available, build the JSON first. The script accepts installed (Desktop app) or web (Web app) as the outer key:

{
  "installed": {
    "client_id": "....apps.googleusercontent.com",
    "project_id": "...",
    "auth_uri": "https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/auth",
    "token_uri": "https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token",
    "auth_provider_x509_cert_url": "https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v1/certs",
    "client_secret": "GOCSPX-...",
    "redirect_uris": ["http://localhost:1"]
  }
}
Then chmod 600 ~/.hermes/google_client_secret.json and delete the loose copy. Never leave a world-readable token behind.

Step 4 — Authorization URL
gsetup --auth-url
Defaults to redirect http://localhost:1; override with GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI. It must be identical for the --auth-url and --auth-code runs, and registered on the client if it is a Web-app client.

Tell the user:

the browser lands on a page saying the site “might be having issues, or it may have moved permanently” — expected; that page is the code carrier
copy the entire address-bar URL, code= included; the address bar keeps it even on the error page
403: access_denied → not a test user (Step 2.5)
redirect_uri_mismatch → URI not registered, or the env var differs between the two calls
Step 5 — Exchange the code
gsetup --auth-code 'http://localhost:1/?state=...&iss=...&code=4/0A...&scope=...'
Quote the argument — a pasted redirect URL contains & and %, which the shell mangles unquoted. Codes are single-use and short-lived: on expiry, mismatch, or a stale tab, regenerate with --auth-url and use only the newest redirect. Never retry the same code.

Step 6 — Verify (never skip)
gsetup --check        # AUTHENTICATED: Token valid at .../google_token.json
gsetup --check-live   # LIVE_CHECK_OK: Real API call succeeded.
gapi gmail labels     # real data proves scopes, not just token presence
Then confirm identity — a token can be valid for the wrong account. Call Gmail users().getProfile(userId="me") and check emailAddress, and state it out loud. Finish with chmod 600 ~/.hermes/google_token.json.

Troubleshooting


Symptom	Cause / fix
python: command not found	No bare python on the box — use the wrappers
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'google'	Ran with system python3.8 — use the venv interpreter
google-auth==2.55.1 unsatisfiable, Python>=3.10	Interpreter too old — run Step 0
redirect_uri_mismatch	Web-app client without that redirect registered, or the env var differs between the two calls
Error 403: access_denied	User not added as a test user on the OAuth consent screen
HttpError 403: Access Not Configured	That API is not enabled on the project
Argument cut at the first &	Redirect URL not quoted
ERROR: No pending OAuth session found	Ran --auth-code without a fresh --auth-url
AUTHENTICATED (partial)	Scopes were deselected on the consent screen — --revoke, redo Steps 4–6
Pitfalls
Never accept a password, app password, or verification code in chat, and never type one into a page. Secrets arrive through the user’s own hands or a vault.
The localhost error page is not a failure — the code is in the URL.
Do not retry an errored code exchange; regenerate the URL instead.
Google emails a “You allowed <app> access to your Google Account data” security alert after consent. The app name is the Cloud project’s consent-screen name, not “Hermes” — recognise it so it is not mistaken for a breach.
After a successful setup, state the account email. Multi-account machines silently authenticate the wrong one.
Using it from another agent
Copy the whole skill directory to the other Hermes install, then run the preflight script there:

scp -r ~/.hermes/skills/productivity/google-oauth-setup user@other-host:~/.hermes/skills/productivity/
# there:
sh ~/.hermes/skills/productivity/google-oauth-setup/scripts/install-google-auth.sh
Nothing in the skill is machine-specific beyond $HERMES_HOME. The OAuth client itself is reusable across machines, but a client still in Testing needs each Google account added as a test user.

