# Google OAuth Setup (bootstrap for the `google-workspace` skill)

Makes the `google-workspace` skill (`gmail`, `calendar`, `drive`, `sheets`, `docs`,
`contacts`) usable on a machine where it is installed but not yet authenticated.
Portable: copy this whole skill directory to another Hermes install and it works.

Scope: getting a working `~/.hermes/google_token.json`. The `google-workspace`
SKILL.md owns command usage; this skill owns the one-time bootstrap.

## Step 0 — Preflight (run this first, always)

The bundled `setup.py` pins `google-auth==2.55.1`, which requires Python >= 3.10.
A machine whose only interpreter is 3.8/3.9 fails every pinned install. Never run
`setup.py` with a bare `python3` — resolve the interpreter first.

```bash
sh "${HERMES_HOME:-$HOME/.hermes}/skills/productivity/google-oauth-setup/scripts/install-google-auth.sh"
```

The script is idempotent and does all of this:

- picks the newest available interpreter (3.13 → 3.10), or asks `$HERMES_HOME/bin/uv`
  to fetch one when none is new enough
- creates `$HERMES_HOME/google-workspace-venv`
- installs the pinned deps via `setup.py --install-deps` run **under that venv**, so the
  pins come from the script itself and cannot drift
- writes `$HERMES_HOME/bin/gsetup` and `$HERMES_HOME/bin/gapi` wrappers that always
  exec the venv interpreter
- prints `gsetup --check`

Prefer the wrappers (`gsetup`, `gapi`) for every later call. If they are missing,
substitute `"$HERMES_HOME/google-workspace-venv/bin/python" <script.py>` — never
system `python3`.

## Step 1 — Triage (ask, do not assume)

1. Which Google services? Email-only does not need this skill — use `himalaya` with a
   Gmail App Password instead (2 minutes, no Cloud project). The setup script here
   always requests the full scope set (gmail read/send/modify, calendar, drive,
   contacts, spreadsheets, documents); it has no `--services` flag.
2. Advanced Protection (hardware security keys)? If yes, the Workspace admin must
   allowlist the OAuth client ID before consent will work.

## Step 2 — Google Cloud OAuth client (user does this; ~5 minutes)

Send these links to the user verbatim:

1. Create/select a project: https://console.cloud.google.com/projectselector2/home/dashboard
2. Enable APIs on THAT project: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/library
   — Gmail API, Google Calendar API, Google Drive API, Google Sheets API,
   Google Docs API, People API. A project is a separate axis from the client: an
   unenabled API fails later with `403: Access Not Configured`.
3. Create credentials: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
   → Create Credentials → OAuth 2.0 Client ID
4. Application type — this decides the redirect story:
   - **Desktop app** (recommended): loopback redirects are allowed automatically.
   - **Web application**: the redirect URI must be registered byte-for-byte, so ask
     the user to add it under the client's *Authorized redirect URIs* and then pass
     that exact value as `GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI`.
5. If the app is still *Testing*, add the account as a test user:
   https://console.cloud.google.com/auth/audience — otherwise consent dies with
   `Error 403: access_denied`.
6. Download the client JSON, or read the ID/secret off the screen.

## Step 3 — Store the client secret

Either path is fine; accept whichever the user gives.

```bash
gsetup --client-secret /path/to/client_secret.json
```

If the user pastes raw values instead of a file, build the JSON yourself first. The
script accepts either `installed` (Desktop app) or `web` (Web app) as the outer key:

```json
{
  "installed": {
    "client_id": "....apps.googleusercontent.com",
    "project_id": "...",
    "auth_uri": "https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/auth",
    "token_uri": "https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token",
    "auth_provider_x509_cert_url": "https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v1/certs",
    "client_secret": "GOCSPX-...",
    "redirect_uris": ["http://localhost:1"]
  }
}
```

Then `chmod 600 $HERMES_HOME/google_client_secret.json` and delete the loose copy.

## Step 4 — Authorization URL

```bash
gsetup --auth-url
```

Defaults to redirect `http://localhost:1`; override with `GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI`.
The value must be identical for the `--auth-url` and `--auth-code` runs, and must be
registered on the client if it is a Web-app client.

Send the user the URL as a single unwrapped line, plus these facts:

- the browser lands on a "this webpage might be having issues, or it may have moved
  permanently" page at `localhost:1` — **expected**, that page is just the code carrier
- copy the ENTIRE address-bar URL, code included; the address bar keeps it even on the
  error page
- `403: access_denied` → they are not a test user (Step 2.5)
- `redirect_uri_mismatch` → the URI is not registered, or `GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI`
  differs between the two calls

## Step 5 — Exchange the code

The code is single-use and short-lived. Quote the argument: a pasted redirect URL
contains `&` and `%`, which the shell mangles unquoted.

```bash
gsetup --auth-code 'http://localhost:1/?state=...&iss=...&code=4/0A...&scope=...'
```

Expect `OK: Authenticated. Token saved to .../google_token.json`. On expiry, mismatch,
or a stale tab, regenerate with `--auth-url` and use only the newest redirect.

## Step 6 — Verify (do not skip, do not trust the exchange's exit code alone)

```bash
gsetup --check        # AUTHENTICATED: Token valid at .../google_token.json
gsetup --check-live   # LIVE_CHECK_OK: Real API call succeeded.
gapi gmail labels     # real data proves scopes, not just token presence
```

Confirm the identity too: build the Gmail `users().getProfile(userId="me")` call and
check `emailAddress` — a token can be valid for the wrong account.
Then `chmod 600 $HERMES_HOME/google_token.json`.

## Wire-up notes

- Token auto-refreshes; nothing to redo. Revoke with `gsetup --revoke`.
- `gws` CLI, if installed, reuses the same `~/.hermes/google_token.json`.
- himalaya: its Gmail backend takes a short-lived bearer token, not the refreshable
  Hermes token. Wire `gmail.auth.token.command` to a helper that prints a fresh access
  token; do not paste the token into the config. Email-only users are usually better off
  with a Gmail App Password there instead.
- Google emails a "You allowed <app> access to your Google Account data" alert after
  consent. The app name is the consent-screen name from the Cloud project, not
  "Hermes" — tell the user that when it arrives.

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause / fix |
|---|---|
| `python: command not found` | No bare `python` on the box — use the wrappers |
| `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'google'` | Ran with system python3.8 — use the venv interpreter |
| `google-auth==2.55.1` unsatisfiable, `Python>=3.10` | Interpreter too old — run Step 0 |
| `redirect_uri_mismatch` | Web-app client without that redirect registered, or env var differs between the two calls |
| `Error 403: access_denied` | User not added as a test user |
| `HttpError 403: Access Not Configured` | That API not enabled on the project |
| Argument cut at the first `&` | Redirect URL not quoted |
| `ERROR: No pending OAuth session found` | Ran `--auth-code` without a fresh `--auth-url` |
| `AUTHENTICATED (partial)` | Scopes were deselected on the consent screen — `--revoke`, redo Steps 4–6 |

## Pitfalls

- Never accept a password/app password/verification code in chat, and never type one
  into a page. Secrets reach the system through the user's own hands or a vault.
- Do not treat the localhost error page as a failure; the code is in the URL.
- Do not retry a code exchange that errored — regenerate the URL instead; codes are
  single-use and a retry can burn a valid one.
- Do not leave the loose client-secret copy or a world-readable token behind.
- State the account email after verification: multi-account machines silently
  authenticate the wrong one.
